      "writtenOn": "2020-11-20T11:19:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think at the very least we have a problem when some of the requests are not of type REQUEST. A typical multilayer request would be [Unmetered, OEM_PAID, Default]. Unmetered, as we discussed before, can\u0027t be a REQUEST because we\u0027ll also need it to include VALIDATED.\n\nSo in this case you can have mSatisfier satisfying the Unmetered request that\u0027s a LISTEN, and then you\u0027ll compare its score to that of a candidate for the OEM_PAID request, which doesn\u0027t make sense.\n\nI think the simplest way to fix this is to replace the isRequest() clause :\n\n  final boolean isKeepingUp \u003d isMultilayeredRequest() || mRequests.get(0).isRequest();\n  for (...) {\n    if (isKeepingUp \u0026\u0026 candidate.satisfies(req)...\n\nWith that changed, I think your reasoning mostly holds, but I think there is another hole. Yes, if candidate.satisfies(req) is true then I think you can be sure that mSatisfier also satisfies the same request (otherwise, candidate would be the satisfier). But its AsValidatedScore can be lower than the current score of mSatisfier, in which case neither of the conditions are true... but we must still return, lest we try to evaluate the candidate for lower-prio requests. So maybe something like :\n\n  ...isKeepingUp \u003d ...\n  for (...) {\n    if (isKeepingUp \u0026\u0026 candidate.satisfies(req)) {\n      return candidate \u003d\u003d mSatisfier || score \u003c candidate.scoreAsValidated\n    }\n  }\n\nAlso, I believe we need careful comments to explain the deductions that led us to the conclusions in this comment thread.",
      "parentUuid": "67de65d7_dbed8612",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 3554,
        "startChar": 35,
